<ChapterLayout
  title="6.3 — Mass Extinctions · AST 100"
  description="The five times life on Earth nearly ended — what caused them, why the speed of change matters more than its size — and the asteroids that still cross our orbit."
  activeId="6.3" chapter={6} current="/chapter/6/6.3">
  <Hero
    tease="Five times in the last 540 million years, most of the species on Earth died in a geological instant. Four of those catastrophes were caused by the planet itself — volcanoes rewriting the atmosphere. One came from space. What all of them share is speed: a world that changes faster than life can adapt is a world that empties. It is the single most important lesson the fossil record has to teach us, and it is not a historical one."
    client:load />

  <article class="topic-page">
    <div class="prose-cosmic lesson-prose">
      <h2 id="bigfive">Five Times the World Nearly Ended</h2>
      <p>
        Extinction is not unusual. It is the normal fate of species — more than 99% of all species that have ever lived
        are gone, and they went at a slow, steady rate, a species here and there, over millions of years. That is the
        background hum of the fossil record.
      </p>
      <p>
        But five times, the hum became a scream. In the last 540 million years — the whole span in which animals have
        existed — the fossil record shows five moments when a huge fraction of all species vanished essentially at once.
        Palaeontologists call them the <strong>Big Five</strong>. Click through them in the figure below, or use the
        arrow keys.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="The Big Five mass extinctions">
        <BigFivePanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <p>
        The worst by an enormous margin was the <strong>End-Permian</strong>, 252 million years ago — nicknamed
        <em>the Great Dying</em>, because roughly nine species in ten disappeared. (Estimates run from 81% to 96%; the
        uncertainty tells you how hard it is to count the dead from a quarter of a billion years away.) Its cause was
        not an asteroid. It was a volcanic eruption in Siberia so vast that it burned through buried coal beds and
        loaded the atmosphere with carbon, cooking the planet and choking the oceans. Life took ten million years to
        recover — the longest hangover in the history of the world.
      </p>
      <p>
        And that points to the pattern most people get backwards. <strong>Four of the five were caused by the Earth
        itself</strong>, mostly by colossal volcanism that changed the atmosphere. Only <em>one</em> — the last one —
        came from space. The famous story is the exception.
      </p>
      <p>
        The exception is worth telling, because it is why you are reading this. Sixty-six million years ago an asteroid
        roughly ten kilometres across struck the shallow sea off what is now Mexico. The impact vaporised rock, threw a
        pall of debris around the world, and shut down photosynthesis — the base of every food chain on the planet.
        The <strong>non-avian dinosaurs</strong>, who had ruled for 180 million years, were gone within a geological
        instant. The small, unpromising mammals in the undergrowth survived, and inherited an emptied world. Every
        primate, including you, is a beneficiary of that rock.
      </p>
      <p>
        What actually does the killing, in all five cases, turns out to be less the <em>size</em> of the environmental
        change than its <strong>speed</strong>. Life adapts remarkably well to a world that warms over a million
        years — species move, evolve, and cope. It fails catastrophically when the same change arrives in a few
        thousand. Ecosystems are outrun. Studies across the whole 450-million-year record find that the <em>rate</em> of
        temperature change predicts extinction severity, independently of how large the change eventually was. That is
        the fossil record's central finding, and it should be read slowly by anyone alive in this century.
      </p>

      <h2 id="neos">The Rocks That Cross Our Path</h2>
      <p>
        The one extinction that came from space raises an obvious question: could it happen again? We inherited a solar
        system littered with leftovers (<a href={subPath("4.1")}>§4.1</a>), and some of their orbits cross ours.
        Astronomers call them <strong>near-Earth objects</strong>, and they sort into four families by orbit shape —
        explore them in the figure below.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="Near-Earth asteroids: the four orbit families">
        <NeoPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <p>
        Only two of the four families actually cross Earth's orbit, and those are the ones planetary defence watches:
        the <strong>Apollos</strong> and the <strong>Atens</strong>. Astronomers have now catalogued tens of thousands
        of near-Earth objects, and — the essential point — they have found nearly all of the <em>large</em> ones, the
        kilometre-scale bodies that could end a civilisation. None of them is on course to hit us. The smaller
        ones, the city-killers, are far more numerous and much harder to spot, and the search continues.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here is what makes this different from every other chapter in the fossil record. The dinosaurs had no telescopes
        and no options. We have both. In September 2022, NASA deliberately flew a spacecraft called <strong>DART</strong>
        into a small, harmless asteroid — and measurably changed its orbit, shortening it by more than half an hour. It
        was the first time in the history of life on Earth that a species has deliberately altered the motion of a
        celestial body to protect itself. For four billion years, the sky did whatever it liked to whatever lived here.
        Now, for the first time, something living here can answer back.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <p><em>Five catastrophes, four of them home-grown, all of them fast. We are the first species with a fossil record
        to read, a telescope to watch with, and a rocket to answer with. Which raises the question this chapter has been
        circling all along: in a Galaxy of four hundred billion stars, is anyone else asking it?</em></p>
    </div>
  </article>

